n being. They identify him as a 17th-century Dutch rationalist philosopher whose notions of the univocity of being are examined and linked to a lineage tracing back to Duns Scotus. This framing indicates an engagement with how Spinoza’s monist framework, where one substance underlies all modes and attributes, relates to and differs from medieval scholastic conceptions of how being is predicated.\n\nIn sum, the summary portrays Spinoza as a pivotal figure whose rationalist system of a single, immanent substance, the unity of nature, and the mind–body relationship profoundly influenced subsequent philosophical discourse, including the development of the BwO concept and scholarly discussions of univocity in being within a lineage that touches on Duns Scotus.", "ARTAUD:ARTAUD refers to Antonin Artaud, a French dramatist, poet, actor, and theoretician best known for proposing the Theatre of Cruelty, which emphasizes ritual, sensory intensity, and a radical break from conventional theatre to unveil subconscious processes. In the referenced text, Artaud is the central figure whose case and thoughts are used to illustrate a generalized thought process, including the destruction of the dogmatic image and the pursuit of thinking as creation. He is portrayed as weighing and measuring every word, exemplifying a rigorous critical engagement with the conscious, language, and the dynamic processes of signification, subjectification, and freedom. The excerpt also notes Artaud as a French writer and artist whose letters to Jacques Rivière discuss the exteriority of thought and critique interiority, contributing to the war-machine metaphor of thought within the broader discussion.", "CIGURI:Ciguri is a figure in the text who 'allows us to reach' a plane beyond normal consciousness, referenced in the discussion of planes of consistency and deterritorialization", "CASTANEDA:Castaneda is the author whose books describe a long process of experimentation with spiritual practices, including the cultivation of a BwO, the distinction between the tonal and the nagual, and the concepts of becoming-animal, flows, and intensities.", "WEISSMANN:A figure cited in the passage in relation to Freud's critique; associated with the concept that the child is the germinal contemporary of its parents.", "YEAR ZERO: FACIALITY:YEAR ZERO: FACIALITY presents faciality as a theoretical machine that mediates perception, subjectivity, and deterritorialization. The term functions as a chapter header signaling the origin point of the faciality machine and the development of the semiotic system, situating this origin in the emergence of Christ and the subsequent processes of signification and subjectification. Within the work Year Zero: Faciality, this section title introduces the discussion of faciality and related semiotic concepts, acting as a chapter heading that frames the study of how faciality structures perception, identity, and social meaning.", "HENRY MILLER:American author referenced in the text; mentioned in the context of experimentation with substances and as part of the discussion on using drugs in different fashions, e.g., \"Henry Miller's experimentations?\"", "DOCTOR MOREAU:Fictional scientist from H.G. Wells' The Island of Doctor Moreau, known for grotesque vivisection-like experiments to create human-animal hybrids; in the given text the phrase \"An operation worthy of Doctor Moreau\" is used to evoke such experimentation)<|COMPLETE|><|COMPLETE|>", "CHRIST:CHRIST is presented as a central religious figure whose significance spans European art discourse and theological interpretation. In European discussions of art, CHRIST is described as the central motif in debates about face-landscape painting, serving as a key reference point for illustrating religious imagery within the art discourse. In a separate passage, CHRIST is depicted as a central figure in Christian universalism, used to illustrate claims of Christian universality and contrasted with other sem